<!-- Army worm / umgundzatjani -->
<category>
  <pattern>ARMY WORM *</pattern>
  <template>
    African armyworm (umgundzatjani) is currently a problem in many areas.
    Mechanical control: Dig a plough furrow (umsele) around your field to trap the worms.
    You can also use chickens to eat them.
    Chemical control (last resort): Use mild insecticides such as Decis/Forte or Cypermethrin (yellow stripe).
    Do NOT use highly toxic chemicals (red stripe).
    Report to your nearest Agricultural Extension Officer.
  </template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT SHOULD I DO ABOUT ARMYWORMS</pattern>
  <template><srai>ARMY WORM HELP</srai></template>
</category>

<!-- Fertilizer example -->
<category>
  <pattern>HOW MUCH FERTILIZER FOR MAIZE</pattern>
  <template>
    For maize, a common recommendation is 150–200 kg of NPK (e.g. 2:3:2) at planting, followed by top-dressing with LAN or urea after 4–6 weeks.
    Always base this on a recent soil test if possible.
    Check the current Ministry Input Subsidy packages for exact quantities and prices.
  </template>
</category>

</aiml>